// Generated by `npm run catalog` from the files in rc/. Do not edit by hand.
// Its own file, so the home page loads it on demand and it stays out of the first download.

/** Each passage's opening paragraph: the text before its last sentence, and that sentence. */
export const RC_LEADS: Record<string, [string, string]> = {
  "econ-cities": ["Economists have long been puzzled by the stubborn growth of large cities. Rents are higher, commutes are longer and congestion eats into every working day, yet people and firms keep arriving. If the costs of urban life are so visible, something less visible must be paying for them.","The usual name for that something is agglomeration: the gains that arise simply because many productive people are close to one another."],
  "econ-thrift": ["Saving is a virtue in a household and, under certain conditions, a hazard in an economy. The reasoning behind this claim, usually called the paradox of thrift, begins with a simple accounting fact: one person's spending is another person's income. If a single family decides to spend less and save more, its savings rise and nobody else notices.","If millions of families make the same decision at once, the shops they no longer visit sell less, lay off staff, and the incomes from which everyone was hoping to save begin to shrink."],
  "econ-rules": ["For most of the twentieth century, the question of how central banks should set interest rates was answered by judgment. Officials looked at the data, consulted their instincts and moved rates as circumstances seemed to require.","The case for discretion was obvious: economies are complicated, shocks are unpredictable, and no rule written in advance can anticipate everything a central banker will face."],
  "econ-lemons": ["Imagine a market for used cars in which sellers know whether their car is reliable but buyers cannot tell. A buyer looking at two shiny hatchbacks of the same model and age has no way of knowing that one has been carefully maintained and the other has a failing gearbox.","Faced with this uncertainty, the sensible buyer offers a price somewhere in the middle, reflecting the average quality of cars on sale."],
  "econ-minwage": ["The textbook prediction about minimum wages is simple. If the price of labour is pushed above what the market would set, employers will buy less of it, so a higher minimum wage should cost some low-paid workers their jobs. Most introductory courses still teach it with a simple diagram of supply and demand, in which any wage floor above the market rate leaves some willing workers without jobs.","For decades most economists accepted this reasoning, and the debate was mainly about how many jobs would be lost."],
  "econ-commons": ["In 1968 the ecologist Garrett Hardin described what he called the tragedy of the commons. Imagine a pasture open to all. Each herder gains the full benefit of adding one more animal but bears only a share of the cost of overgrazing, so each keeps adding animals until the pasture is ruined. Hardin concluded that shared resources must be either divided into private property or controlled by the state.","The argument seemed to explain everything from emptied fisheries to polluted rivers, and his essay became one of the most cited in the social sciences."],
  "phil-theseus": ["The ancient puzzle of the ship of Theseus goes like this. The ship in which the hero returned to Athens was kept as a memorial, and as its planks rotted they were replaced one by one, until no original timber remained. Was it still the same ship? The puzzle sharpens if we imagine that someone collected the discarded planks and assembled them into a second ship.","Now there are two candidates for the title, and each has a claim: one has continuity of form and history, the other has all the original material."],
  "phil-luck": ["Two drivers have a drink too many at the same party and drive home along similar roads. Both are equally careless; both drift across the line at the same bend. On one road a child steps out, and on the other the road is empty. The first driver kills the child and the second arrives home without incident. Most of us judge the first driver far more harshly, and so does the law.","Yet the only difference between them is something neither controlled."],
  "phil-experts": ["Most of what any of us believes about the world we have not checked for ourselves. I believe that the earth orbits the sun, that smoking causes cancer and that a city I have never visited exists, but I could not produce the evidence for any of these if asked. I rely on testimony: on teachers, textbooks, doctors and the long chain of people who stand behind them.","Philosophers once treated this dependence as a weakness, a shortcut that the truly rational person would avoid by thinking everything through alone."],
  "psy-planning": ["Ask people how long a task will take and they will usually give an answer that is too short. Students underestimate how long their essays will take, homeowners underestimate the cost of renovations, and governments underestimate the cost of bridges and railways, often by staggering margins.","The psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky called this the planning fallacy: the tendency to predict that one's own plans will go better than similar plans have gone in the past."],
  "psy-memory": ["We tend to picture memory as a recording: an experience is stored somewhere in the brain and, when we remember, the recording is played back, perhaps a little faded. A century of research has made this picture hard to defend.","Remembering appears to be less like playing a tape than like reconstructing a scene from fragments, with the gaps filled in by general knowledge, expectation and the questions we are asked."],
  "psy-choice-blind": ["In a study published in 2005, researchers showed volunteers pairs of photographs of faces and asked them to choose the more attractive one. After each choice, the experimenter slid the chosen card across the table and asked the volunteer to explain the preference. On some trials, using a simple card trick, the experimenter handed over the face the volunteer had rejected. Most of the time, no one noticed.","More striking still, volunteers went on to explain why they preferred a face they had not chosen, sometimes citing features, such as earrings, that only the rejected face had."],
  "psy-willpower": ["In a well-known experiment from the late 1990s, hungry volunteers sat in a room that smelled of freshly baked cookies. Some were allowed to eat the cookies; others had to eat radishes instead and leave the cookies untouched. Afterwards, all of them worked on a puzzle that, unknown to them, could not be solved. Those who had resisted the cookies gave up considerably sooner. The effect was large, and the design was easy to copy.","The researchers proposed that self-control draws on a limited resource, which is used up by one act of restraint and leaves less for the next."],
  "phil-future": ["Most of us believe we can wrong people who do not yet exist. A government that buries nuclear waste in a way that will leak in three hundred years seems to act badly towards the people living then, even though none of them is alive today. Much of what we think we owe the future rests on cases like this.","The philosopher Derek Parfit showed that explaining the belief is surprisingly difficult."],
  "phil-sentience": ["Until recently, the animal welfare laws of many countries protected only vertebrates: mammals, birds, reptiles, amphibians and fish. Crabs, lobsters and octopuses fell outside them, which is why a lobster could legally be boiled alive in places where doing the same to a chicken would be a crime.","The line was drawn less by evidence than by habit, and by the difficulty of knowing what, if anything, such creatures feel."],
  "psy-anchoring": ["In a classic experiment from the 1970s, Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman spun a wheel of fortune in front of volunteers. The wheel was rigged to stop at either 10 or 65, and the volunteers knew it had nothing to do with what followed. They were then asked whether the percentage of African countries in the United Nations was higher or lower than that number, and then to estimate the true figure. Those who had seen 10 gave a median estimate of 25 per cent; those who had seen 65 gave 45 per cent.","A number they knew to be random had pulled their judgement towards it."],
  "psy-marshmallow": ["In the late 1960s and early 1970s, the psychologist Walter Mischel offered preschool children at a Stanford nursery a choice. They could eat one treat, such as a marshmallow, straight away, or wait alone in a room for up to twenty minutes and receive two. Years later, Mischel and his colleagues followed up some of the children and reported that those who had waited longer went on to do better at school and to cope better with stress as adolescents.","The marshmallow test became a famous symbol of self-control, and of the idea that the ability to delay gratification in early childhood predicts success in life."],
  "sci-shrinking": ["In the nineteenth century the biologist Carl Bergmann noticed that, within groups of related warm-blooded animals, those living in colder places tend to be larger. A large body has less surface area relative to its volume than a small one, so it loses heat more slowly; in the cold, size is an advantage, and in the heat a smaller body sheds warmth more easily.","The pattern, now called Bergmann's rule, holds loosely but widely, from deer to sparrows."],
  "sci-wood": ["Beneath a forest floor, the roots of many trees are wrapped in threads of fungi. The relationship, called mycorrhiza, is ancient and usually beneficial to both sides: the fungus gathers water and minerals from the soil and passes some to the tree, which in return feeds the fungus sugars made by photosynthesis.","Because a single fungus can connect to several trees, and a single tree to several fungi, ecologists began in the 1990s to describe forests as linked by underground networks."],
  "sci-placebo": ["In a well-designed drug trial, some patients receive the drug and others a placebo, an inactive substance made to look identical. Neither the patients nor the doctors treating them know who has received which. The point of this arrangement is not only to see whether the drug works but to measure how much better it works than the simple experience of being treated.","That second quantity is often surprisingly large."],
  "sci-microbiome": ["Each of us carries tens of trillions of microbes in the gut, a community whose cells roughly match the number of human cells in the body. For most of medical history these organisms were treated mainly as a source of infection. Over the past two decades, cheap genetic sequencing has made it possible to catalogue them in detail, and the results have changed how scientists think about the body.","The gut's microbes help digest fibre, make vitamins, train the immune system and keep harmful species in check."],
  "sci-leapsecond": ["For most of history, time was defined by the sky. A day was one turn of the Earth, and the second was a fraction of that day. In 1967 scientists cut this link, redefining the second by the vibrations of caesium atoms, which are far steadier than the planet's spin.","The best atomic clocks now keep time so precisely that they would drift by less than a second over many millions of years."],
  "sci-dinobirds": ["In 1861, just two years after Charles Darwin published On the Origin of Species, quarry workers in Bavaria found a fossil that seemed made to order for his theory. Archaeopteryx had feathers and wings like a bird, but also teeth, clawed fingers and a long bony tail like a small reptile. Only a dozen or so specimens have been found since, each treated as a treasure.","It looked like a creature caught halfway between two groups, and it set off a debate about where birds came from that lasted for more than a century."],
  "sci-ozone": ["In 1985 three British scientists reported that the ozone layer over Antarctica was thinning dramatically each spring. Ozone high in the atmosphere absorbs most of the sun's ultraviolet radiation, which damages DNA and causes skin cancer and cataracts. The cause was soon traced to chlorofluorocarbons, or CFCs, gases used in refrigerators, aerosol sprays and foam.","Stable and harmless at ground level, CFCs drift into the stratosphere, where ultraviolet light breaks them apart and releases chlorine that destroys ozone."],
  "soc-algorithms": ["When a bank uses software to decide who gets a loan, or a city uses it to decide where to send police, the decision is often defended as objective. A machine, the argument goes, has no prejudices. It applies the same rules to everyone, unlike a human officer who may be tired, hurried or biased.","This defence contains a truth and hides a confusion."],
  "soc-remote": ["For most of history, where you lived largely decided the work you could do. The pandemic briefly loosened that link. Millions of office workers discovered that much of their job could be done from a spare room, and some never went back.","The shift has prompted predictions that remote work will spread opportunity away from expensive cities, letting a programmer in a small town earn a big-city salary while paying a small-town rent."],
  "soc-repair": ["A generation ago a broken radio went to a repair shop. Today a broken phone, kettle or printer is more often thrown away. The change is familiar to anyone over forty, and it is often lamented. It is tempting to explain this by a decline in values, a culture that prefers novelty to care.","The economics of repair offers a less moralising account."],
  "soc-cash": ["For much of the history of aid, the idea of simply handing money to poor people was treated with suspicion. Donors worried that cash would be spent on alcohol or tobacco, or that it would make people less willing to work. Behind these worries lay an assumption, rarely stated, that poverty reflects poor judgement as much as a lack of money.","Aid therefore usually arrived in kind, as food, livestock, training or buildings chosen by outsiders who believed they knew what the poor needed."],
  "soc-streets": ["In 1961 the writer and activist Jane Jacobs published a book that attacked the planners then rebuilding American cities. Their ideal was order: homes in one zone, offices in another, shops in a third, with wide roads and open lawns between towers. Many of the neighbourhoods Jacobs defended were labelled slums and marked for clearance.","Jacobs, who lived on a busy street in Greenwich Village, argued that the planners' tidy vision destroyed the very things that made cities work."],
  "soc-corrections": ["In 2010 two political scientists reported a troubling result. When people who held a false belief were shown a correction, some of them held the belief more firmly than before. The finding, soon called the backfire effect, seemed to explain a great deal about politics and the internet: facts, it appeared, could not only fail to persuade but actively entrench error.","Journalists and fact-checkers began to wonder whether their work was doing more harm than good."],
  "lit-translation": ["A common complaint about translations is that something is always lost. The Italian proverb puts it more harshly: traduttore, traditore, translator, traitor.","Behind the complaint lies a picture of translation as the transfer of fixed content from one container to another, in which the best a translator can do is to spill as little as possible."],
  "lit-minds": ["Novels let us do something that ordinary life does not: overhear another person's thoughts. In conversation we infer what others think from what they say and do; in fiction we are often told directly, or something close to it.","The most distinctive technique for this, developed by writers such as Jane Austen and Gustave Flaubert, is known as free indirect style."],
  "lit-tragedy": ["Tragedy poses an old puzzle. In ordinary life we avoid suffering and do not seek out the sight of it. Yet for two and a half thousand years audiences have paid to watch kings blind themselves, mothers lose their children and lovers die through misunderstanding, and they leave the theatre saying they enjoyed it. What is the pleasure in pain that is only pretended?","The question was already old when Aristotle took it up, and it has never quite been settled."],
  "hist-maps": ["A map looks like a picture of the world, but it is better understood as an argument about it. Every map selects what to show and what to leave out, chooses a centre and a scale, and names places in someone's language.","These choices are rarely neutral, and in the age of European empires they often did political work long before any soldier arrived."],
  "hist-print": ["It has become a commonplace that the printing press changed everything. After Johannes Gutenberg began printing with movable type in Mainz around 1450, the story goes, books became cheap, literacy spread, the Reformation became unstoppable and modern science became possible.","The story is not wrong, but historians have spent decades complicating it, and the complications are instructive."],
  "hist-cholera": ["In the late summer of 1854, cholera killed more than five hundred people in ten days in the Soho district of London. The disease was terrifying: it could kill a healthy adult within a day, through violent diarrhoea and dehydration. The dominant medical theory of the time held that the disease spread through miasma, a poisonous vapour rising from rotting matter, which is why the city's response focused on clearing filth and improving ventilation.","The physician John Snow believed instead that cholera was carried in water contaminated by the waste of the sick."],
  "hist-silk": ["The phrase 'Silk Road' was coined only in 1877, by the German geographer Ferdinand von Richthofen, whose work in China included surveying routes for a proposed railway. It has since become one of the most evocative names in history, conjuring caravans carrying bolts of silk across deserts from Chang'an to Rome. The name caught on slowly, and became popular only in the twentieth century, through travel writing, exhibitions and, later, television.","The image is not wrong so much as misleading, and the ways in which it misleads are instructive."],
  "lit-oral": ["For centuries readers wondered how a single poet could have composed the Iliad and the Odyssey, poems of more than twenty-seven thousand lines between them, without writing. Some scholars doubted that one person had composed them at all, and argued that they were stitched together from shorter songs by later editors.","The question was transformed in the 1930s by Milman Parry, a young American scholar, and his assistant Albert Lord, who travelled to Yugoslavia to record singers of heroic songs in coffee houses and villages."],
  "lit-detective": ["In 1929 the priest and crime writer Ronald Knox published ten commandments for detective fiction. The criminal must be mentioned early in the story; no more than one secret room or passage is allowed; the detective must not commit the crime himself; and no clue may be hidden from the reader. Knox wrote partly in jest, but his list captured a real ambition of the period's writers.","A detective story, in what is now called its golden age, was meant to be a fair contest between author and reader, in which a careful reader could, in principle, solve the crime before the final chapter."],
  "hist-calendar": ["The calendar that Julius Caesar introduced in 46 BCE assumed that a year lasts exactly 365 and a quarter days, and added a leap day every fourth year to keep pace. The true solar year is about eleven minutes shorter.","The error is tiny, but it accumulates: by the sixteenth century the calendar had drifted about ten days away from the seasons, and the spring equinox, which the Church used to fix the date of Easter, was arriving around 11 March instead of 21 March."],
};
